<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch9-section-isobaric-coordinates-subsection-total-derivative" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Total derivative</title>

    <p>
        We proceed similarly to the derivation of the total derivative presented in
        <xref ref="ch1-section-calculus-subsection-total-differentiation" text="custom">Chapter 1</xref>
        by considering a state variable <m>f = f(x,y,p,t)</m> measured within an air parcel
        (again with the reminder that <m>f</m> could be replaced by a vector quantity).
        If the air parcel's position changes with time as it moves through the atmosphere,
        then <m>x = x(t)</m>, <m>y = y(t)</m>, and <m>p = p(t)</m>, and so <m>f = f(t)</m>.
        Then, as in Chapter 1, the chain rule for multivaiable functions produces
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-total-derivative-isobaric-scalar-start" number="yes">
            \frac{df}{dt}
            =
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial t}
            + \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{dt}
            + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{dt}
            + \frac{\partial f}{\partial p}\frac{dp}{dt}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Again,
        <m>u = \dfrac{dx}{dt}</m> and <m>v = \dfrac{dy}{dt}</m> are the zonal and meridional components
        of the horizontal wind vector <m>\vec{v} = u\hat{i} + v\hat{j}</m>,
        respectively.
        We define the <term>pressure vertical velocity</term> ("<term>omega</term>") as
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-omega" number="yes">
            \omega = \frac{dp}{dt}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        which describes an air parcel's vertical motion in terms of the time rate of change of its pressure [<m>\text{Pa} \, \text{s}^{-1}</m>].
        This is in contrast to the "usual" vertical velocity <m>w = \dfrac{dz}{dt}</m>,
        which describes an air parcel's vertical motion in terms of the time rate of change of its height [<m>\text{m} \, \text{s}^{-1}</m>].
    </p>

    <p>
        Then with <m>\dfrac{Df}{Dt}</m> substituted for <m>\dfrac{df}{dt}</m>
        as was done in <xref ref="ch1-section-calculus-subsection-total-differentiation" text="custom">Chapter 1</xref>,
        <xref ref="eqn-total-derivative-isobaric-scalar-start" text="type-global" /> becomes:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-total-derivative-isobaric-scalar" number="yes">
            \frac{Df}{Dt}
            =
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial t}
            + u \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
            + v \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
            + \omega \frac{\partial f}{\partial p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        which could be written as
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            \frac{Df}{Dt}
            =
            \left(
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial t}
            \right)_{x,y,p}
            + u
            \left(
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
            \right)_{y,p,t}
            + v
            \left(
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
            \right)_{x,p,t}
            + \omega
            \left(
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial p}
            \right)_{x,y,t}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        or
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            \frac{Df}{Dt}
            =
            \left(
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial t}
                + u
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
                + v
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
            \right)_p
            + \omega
            \left(
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial p}
            \right)_{x,y,t}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        with subscripts to emphasize the variables that are held constant for each partial derivative.
        However,
        most often the subscripts are omitted for brevity,
        and one must note the use of <m>\omega</m> rather than <m>w</m> to realize they are looking at the total derivative in isobaric coordinates.
    </p>

    <p>
        The total derivative in isobaric coordinates
        <xref ref="eqn-total-derivative-isobaric-scalar" text="type-global" />
        is written in vector form as
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-total-derivative-isobaric-vector" number="yes">
            \frac{Df}{Dt}
            =
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial t}
            + \vec{v} \cdot \nabla_p f
            + \omega \frac{\partial f}{\partial p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Note that, while <m>w \gt 0</m> for rising motion and <m>w \lt 0</m> for sinking motion,
        <m>\omega \lt 0</m> for rising motion and <m>\omega \gt 0</m> for sinking motion.
        This is because,
        as an air parcel rises,
        its pressure lowers as it encounters lower pressure environments since <m>p</m> decreases with increasing height in Earth's atmosphere.
        Therefore,
        <m>\omega \lt 0</m> for rising motion while <m>w \gt 0</m>,
        and <m>\omega \gt 0</m> for sinking motion while <m>w \lt 0</m>.
    </p>

</subsection>